WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.776 line:92%,end size:91%
As a cell divides, where does each chromosome go?

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.750 line:92%,end size:91%
Live cells lack a general way to label chromosome
identities stably through mitosis.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:16.957 line:92%,end size:91%
In fixed cells, G-banding reveals a recognizable
pattern of light and dark bands.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:24.346 line:92%,end size:91%
Rehydrated chromosomes retain more of their shape.
Without the bands, their identities are harder to read.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:32.100 line:92%,end size:91%
Fluorescence in situ hybridization, or FISH, uses a
known DNA sequence to establish a reference identity.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:39.344 line:92%,end size:91%
ChromoPix learns from both banded and bandless images
to segment chromosomes and predict their identities.

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:48.934 line:92%,end size:91%
Follow this one chromosome. We measure its relative
area, elongation, solidity, circularity, and
tortuosity.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:55.487 line:92%,end size:91%
UMAP puts those measurements on a two-dimensional map.
One point is one chromosome.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:04.873 line:92%,end size:91%
Now pull back. Across more than seven hundred thousand
instances, chromosomes organize by shape. Color marks
chromosome class.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:10.869 line:92%,end size:91%
The G-banding and FISH maps show a similar order across
chromosome classes.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.947 line:92%,end size:91%
That agreement gives us a reference for interpreting
chromosomes in living cells.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:25.309 line:92%,end size:91%
ChromoPose separates chromosomes in three dimensions.
Tracking links their instances as the cell divides.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:32.767 line:92%,end size:91%
ChromoLift learns how a flat projection relates to the
measured shape of the same three-dimensional
chromosome.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:40.839 line:92%,end size:91%
ChromoID compares a live chromosome with a reference
library, using several shape features together.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:48.700 line:92%,end size:91%
A withheld fluorescent tag provides an independent
check on the predicted identity.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:55.500 line:92%,end size:91%
In patient-derived tumor cells, ChromoWorld detected an
unusual pattern of chromosome fourteen segregation.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:00.781 line:92%,end size:91%
The images suggested that one daughter received two
copies and the other received four.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.780 line:92%,end size:91%
We isolated individual daughter cells by
micromanipulation and grew them into separate clones.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:11.461 line:92%,end size:91%
The copy-number differences persisted across passages.

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:18.690 line:92%,end size:91%
Higher-copy clones showed stronger AKT signaling and
greater sensitivity to capivasertib ex vivo.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:24.220 line:92%,end size:91%
The clinical team independently chose capivasertib with
endocrine therapy.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:30.021 line:92%,end size:91%
At eight weeks, liver target lesions had shrunk nearly
a third. By eighteen weeks, nearly half.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:35.216 line:92%,end size:91%
Serum tumor markers fell, and measures of liver
function improved.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.681 line:92%,end size:91%
Daily function improved, with less liver pain and
fatigue.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:45.928 line:92%,end size:91%
Watching a cell divide can point to a treatment
hypothesis worth testing.
